# 100. Same Tree

## APPROACH: SAME TREE (RECURSIVE / DFS)

Idea:
Do binary trees identical tabhi hote hain jab:
1. Dono ka structure exactly same ho.
2. Har corresponding node ki value same ho.
Dono trees ko saath-saath recursively traverse karte hain. Agar kisi bhi point par structure ya value mismatch ho jaye, toh answer `false` hoga.

--------------------------------------------------

APPROACH (Recursive / DFS)

1. Base Cases (Structural Identity Check):
   - Agar dono nodes `null` hain (`p == null && q == null`):
     Dono trees yahan tak identical hain -> `return true`.
   - Agar unme se koi ek `null` hai aur doosra nahi (`p == null || q == null`):
     Structure match nahi hua -> `return false`.

2. Value & Subtree Check:
   - Check karo ki current node ki value match karti hai ya nahi: `p.val == q.val`.
   - Recursively left subtrees compare karo: `isSameTree(p.left, q.left)`.
   - Recursively right subtrees compare karo: `isSameTree(p.right, q.right)`.

3. Agar teeno conditions true hain, tabhi trees identical hain:
   `return (p.val == q.val) && isSameTree(p.left, q.left) && isSameTree(p.right, q.right);`

--------------------------------------------------

EXAMPLE

Tree 1 (p):        Tree 2 (q):
    1                  1
   / \                / \
  2   3              2   3

Dry Run:
- Root level:
  p(1) != null && q(1) != null
  p.val == q.val (1 == 1) -> Match!

- Check Left:
  p(2) != null && q(2) != null
  p.val == q.val (2 == 2) -> Match!
  - 2 ke left null == null -> true
  - 2 ke right null == null -> true
  - Left subtree returns true

- Check Right:
  p(3) != null && q(3) != null
  p.val == q.val (3 == 3) -> Match!
  - 3 ke left null == null -> true
  - 3 ke right null == null -> true
  - Right subtree returns true

- Final Result: true && true && true = true

--------------------------------------------------

WHY THIS BASE CASE ORDER?

- `if (p == null && q == null) return true;` pehle likhna zaroori hai.
- Agar yeh false hota hai aur fir `if (p == null || q == null)` chalta hai, iska guarantee matlab hota hai ki dono me se exactly ek hi `null` hai, jo direct structural mismatch prove karta hai.

--------------------------------------------------

COMPLEXITY

N = Number of nodes in the smaller tree

Time:
O(N) -> Har node pair ko at most ek baar compare karte hain. Short-circuit evaluation mismatch milte hi execution rok deta hai.

Space:
O(H) -> Recursive call stack height ke barabar memory leta hai.
Worst case (skewed tree): O(N)
Best/Average case (balanced tree): O(log N)

--------------------------------------------------

INTERVIEW POINT

- Interviewer follow-up: *"Can you solve this iteratively?"*
  - **Iterative Approach:** Ek `Queue<TreeNode>` use karo aur elements ko pairs me push karo: `queue.offer(p); queue.offer(q);`. Har iteration me 2 nodes nikaal kar identical checks perform karo.
- Closely related problems:
  1. Symmetric Tree (LeetCode 101) - wahan `p.left` ko `q.right` ke saath aur `p.right` ko `q.left` ke saath compare karte hain (mirror image).
  2. Subtree of Another Tree (LeetCode 572) - jisme har node par yahi `isSameTree` function call hota hai.

